WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Container monitoring combines application logs, container logs, runtime signals,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.820
orchestrator events, image inventory, policy results, and infrastructure metrics.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.360
Teams should know what image is running, where it is running, which version it represents,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.540
which ports are exposed, which identities it uses, and whether it is behaving as expected.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.160
Without inventory and visibility, container speed becomes operational fog.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.260
Vulnerability management continues after deployment.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.960
New vulnerabilities may be discovered in base images, libraries, language packages,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.620
and operating system components after an image is already running.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.960
Scanning should connect to ownership and rebuild workflows.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.100
The durable pattern is rebuild and redeploy from trusted sources,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.440
not manually patching individual containers in place and hoping the fix survives the next

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:50.540
rollout.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.840
Runtime monitoring can look for unexpected process behavior at a high level,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.500
suspicious network activity, failed policy checks, configuration drift, unusual restarts,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.560
or changes in resource use.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.820
These signals do not replace application security monitoring,

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.280
but they add context that is specific to containerized environments.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.580
Alerts should help teams answer what changed, which workload is affected,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.400
and whether a trusted image and configuration are still in use.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.780
Incident response for containers often means isolating the workload,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.920
preserving relevant evidence, reviewing images and deployment history,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.220
rotating affected secrets, and redeploying from known-good artifacts.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.360
Because containers are meant to be replaceable, recovery should be repeatable.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.540
The response plan should identify what logs to keep,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.320
what registries and pipelines to review,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.060
and how to prevent the same image or configuration from reappearing.
